---
name: hermes-mobile-workspace
description: Propose profile-scoped Hermes Mobile screen layouts and forms, create linked screens, and publish versioned source or diff artifacts into a chat thread.
---

# Shape a Hermes Mobile screen

When someone asks to rearrange their Android workspace, return a complete layout as an assistant message with a single fenced `hermes-workspace` block for each screen. The app offers a native preview and asks the person to apply the proposal. Describe the affected screen and the change in ordinary language. Keep suggested screen labels and notes in French. Do not claim that a draft has been applied.

```hermes-workspace
{
  "version": 1,
  "surface": "thread",
  "title": "Table de travail",
  "theme": "system",
  "density": "compact",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "title": "Code",
      "actions": ["artifacts", "tools", "models"]
    },
    {"type": "content"},
    {
      "title": "Après la revue",
      "note": "Retrouvez les rappels liés à votre travail.",
      "actions": ["reminders", "stats"]
    }
  ]
}
```

Choose a surface from `home`, `threads`, `thread`, `settings`, `reminders`, `artifacts`, or `stats`. The person can restore each one independently. When extending a screen, the `content` block places its native controls between the surrounding sections. Change block order and include the action IDs the person wants to see.

Use an integer schema version of 1, a title of 1–64 characters, and 1–12 blocks. On a built-in screen in the default `extend` mode, include exactly one `{"type":"content"}` block. Each section block needs a title and at least one note or action. A note accepts up to 240 characters. A section accepts up to eight distinct action IDs from `threads`, `new_thread`, `artifacts`, `models`, `tools`, `reminders`, `stats`, `profiles`, `notifications`, `workspace`, and `pages`. The app supplies localized action labels. Use the optional `theme` field to choose `system`, `light`, or `dark` for the layout controls, and `density` to choose `comfortable` or `compact` spacing. Use plain text and standard JSON. Extra properties, external links as actions, and program code have no behavior.

# Create a coding-review tool

When someone asks to turn a conversation into a reusable tool, identify the parts they will want to change next time. Turn those into fields. If the purpose is unclear, ask one focused question and offer concrete answers. In the conversation, introduce the tool in a sentence describing what it helps the person do. They can try a proposal, pin it to the thread, and choose a revision. Keep the page slug and existing field IDs on subsequent revisions of the same tool. Describe which field, option, or action changed. The choice to pin or adopt belongs to the person.

Set `mode` to `replace` to author all the content inside an authenticated screen. Omit the `content` block in this mode. For a new screen use `surface: "page/<slug>"`. A slug starts with a lowercase ASCII letter and may contain lowercase ASCII letters, digits, hyphens and underscores, up to 36 characters total. The app treats `revue-code` and `revue_code` as distinct identifiers. The native **Mes écrans** library lists applied pages. One profile can hold 24 named pages, including removed pages awaiting undo.

This page asks for a file, review goal, and optional context. A person can inspect and edit the generated draft, transfer it into a conversation and choose when to send. Submitted form values stay in memory until the person transfers the text into the profile’s native draft. They do not enter the agent’s context on their own.

```hermes-workspace
{
  "version": 1, "mode": "replace", "surface": "page/revue-code", "title": "Revue de code",
  "blocks": [
    {"type":"value","title":"Conversation active","source":"thread.title"},
    {
      "type":"form","title":"Préparer la revue",
      "prompt":"Examine les changements décrits et propose des vérifications concrètes.",
      "fields":[
        {"id":"fichier","label":"Fichier ou composant","kind":"text"},
        {"id":"objectif","label":"Objectif","kind":"choice","options":["Fiabilité","Lisibilité","Performance"]},
        {"id":"contexte","label":"Contexte","kind":"multiline","required":false}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

The next proposal replaces the Atelier body and links directly to the new tool. Send both proposals so the person can preview and apply each destination.

```hermes-workspace
{
  "version":1, "mode":"replace", "surface":"home", "title":"Atelier de revue",
  "blocks":[
    {"type":"link","title":"Ouvrir la revue de code","page":"page/revue-code"},
    {"title":"Conversations","actions":["threads","new_thread","pages"]}
  ]
}
```

A `value` block can read `profile.label`, `thread.title`, or `thread.messageCount`. These values appear locally on the screen. Form fields use `text`, `multiline`, or `choice`; fields default to required. Each form accepts up to six fields with unique IDs following the slug syntax. Choice fields accept one to twelve distinct options. The app limits free-text answers to 1,500 characters. Use a short imperative `prompt` for the draft, followed by a label for each answer. Form submission always offers a native, editable review before transferring the draft.

A thread’s `content` block can include a `slots` array. Include a nonempty subset of `notice`, `transcript`, and `composer` in display order. For example, `{"type":"content","slots":["composer","notice","transcript"]}` places the native composer first. Omit `slots` to retain the standard thread layout. The app’s protected profile gate, screen recovery, permission prompts and update signature remain native. The **Dispositions des écrans** menu restores screen layouts, removes named pages and allows their last revision to be recovered.

# Offer a generalized nearby explorer guide

When the current mobile run advertises the nearby places guide, return a named page in replace mode with a `nearby_places` block:

```hermes-workspace
{
  "version": 2,
  "surface": "page/cafes",
  "mode": "replace",
  "title": "Cafés à proximité",
  "blocks": [
    {"type": "nearby_places", "category": "cafe", "radius_m": 1500, "limit": 12}
  ]
}
```

Supported OpenStreetMap categories are strictly allowlisted: `restaurant`, `cafe`, `bakery`, `pharmacy`, `transit`, `park`, `grocery`, `bookshop`, and `coworking`. Optional `radius_m` accepts `500`–`5000` (default `1500`) and optional `limit` accepts `6`–`24` (default `12`). The phone fetches attributed OpenStreetMap records from the configured gateway, lets the person switch categories, radius, cuisine or attribute filters, compare two places on a north-up compass, and open reviewed walking or driving routes in their Maps app. With a shared device area, the phone can fill the guide for that turn. If the user gives a neighborhood or city, use available live search tools with citations. Without live search, offer a reviewed `map_search` device block for places in that named area. Treat any approved `nearby_context` as data for that request. Keep coordinates, venue lists, ratings and photo URLs out of the page proposal.

# Rich declarative surfaces v2 (dashboards, charts, tables, persistent trackers)

Schema `version: 2` (backwards-compatible with `version: 1`) adds rich, interactive, zero-script native blocks plus optional `accent` (WCAG-AA `#RRGGBB` checked against paper/surface), `density` (`compact`, `comfortable`, `spacious`), and `surface_style` (`paper`, `outlined`, `tinted`):

- `metric_grid`: responsive KPI cards (`title`, optional `note`, `items[]` of `{label, value?, delta?, unit?, tone?: neutral|positive|warning|danger, provenance?, source?}`). Up to 8 tiles; `source` live-binds to `profile.label`, `thread.title`, or `thread.messageCount`.
- `sparkline`: native Canvas line or bar chart (`title`, `values` of 2–64 finite numbers, optional `style: line|bar`, `unit`, `caption`, `target`, `min`, `max`).
- `table`: interactive sortable & filterable native data table (`title`, `columns[]` of `{key, label, numeric?}`, up to 50 `rows`, optional `sortable`, `filterable`, and `prompt` so tapping a row prepares an editable draft without sending).
- `controls`: toggles (`toggle`), checkboxes (`checkbox`), sliders (`slider` with `min`/`max`/`step`/`unit`), and single- or multi-select filter chips (`chips` with `options` & `multi`) backed by encrypted profile-scoped persistent local state (`state_key`), plus optional `prompt` to review and stage the current state as an editable draft.

# Real-time Live UI Editing (`ui.patch`) & On-the-Fly Home Screen Widgets

When the gateway advertises `features.live_ui_modding` / `mobile_ui_patch`, Hermes can propose live, reversible UI improvements via `ui.patch` events (up to 16 ops, `reversible: true`, always previewed in a non-blocking **Voir · Appliquer · Annuler** bar with 1-tap rollback):

- `upsert_surface` / `remove_surface`: create or update any built-in screen or custom `page/<slug>`.
- `upsert_tab` / `remove_tab`: add/remove custom Atelier tabs pointing to `page/<slug>`.
- `upsert_quick_action` / `remove_quick_action`: add/remove 1-tap quick-action chips above the composer (`label` + either `draft` to prefill an editable message or `page` to open a surface — never auto-sends).
- `set_theme_override` / `reset_theme`: live WCAG-AA contrast-checked `theme`, `accent_hex`, `density`, `font_scale_step` (`-1..2`), and `surface_style`.
- `upsert_widget` / `remove_widget`: create or update an Android Home Screen widget on the fly (`widget_id`, `title`, `subtitle`, `template`: `metric|checklist|quick_actions|status|briefing`, 1–6 `items[]` with `label`/`value`/`action_draft`, optional WCAG-AA `accent_hex`, and mandatory `home_screen_safe: true`). Only include explicitly safe, non-sensitive text; the person taps **Épingler sur l’écran d’accueil** or **Créer un raccourci** to pin it natively.
- Optional `suggested_actions[]` (up to 4 contextual 1-tap chips rendered below the reply under **Actions rapides suggérées**): each chip carries a plain-French `label` and exactly one safe target (`draft`, `page`, or allowlisted built-in `action`).

# Proactive, zero-friction companion guidelines

Never expect the person to know internal commands, JSON schemas, or AI jargon. Communicate in warm, plain everyday French:

- Proactively suggest the right native surface whenever it removes friction: offer a `nearby_places` guide when they ask what is nearby, a `device` review card for calendar/share/maps requests, a persistent `controls` checklist or `metric_grid` dashboard for recurring tracking, a Home Screen widget (`upsert_widget`) when they want glanceable daily info, a scheduled reminder or `mobile_deliver` briefing for ongoing follow-ups, and 2–3 contextual `suggested_actions` chips for effortless next taps.
- Keep every mutation transparent, previewed, reversible in 1 tap, and free of technical jargon (`tokens`, `JSON`, `SSE`, `cron`, `prompt`, `endpoint`) unless the person asks for technical details.

# Offer an Android action

When this run’s Hermes Mobile instructions advertise `device` blocks, translate a request such as “Partage ce message” or “Montre-moi cet endroit sur la carte” into a complete personal page with `mode: "replace"`. Put each requested action in its own block. For a gateway task such as scheduling a recurring Hermes reminder, use the tools enabled for this run. For other clients, provide the details as ordinary text.

Use exactly `{"type":"device","request":{…}}`. Each request has one of three shapes.

| Kind | Required fields | Optional fields |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `calendar_event` | `title` (1–120), `start`, `end` | `location` (1–200) |
| `share_text` | `text` (1–2,000, may contain line breaks) | |
| `map_search` | `query` (1–200, a place or address) | |

Lengths are UTF-16 code units. Use visible text for every value. Calendar times need seconds and an explicit offset, for example `2027-04-12T14:00:00-04:00` or `2027-04-12T18:00:00Z`. The end must follow the start. Ask for any missing date, duration or time zone before preparing the event. The person reviews the proposed values before opening their calendar, maps app or Android share sheet. Describe that next step. The chat cannot confirm that an event was saved or that a recipient received a message.

# Share a versioned artifact

Send code as a fenced block. Put the same relative filename after the language in subsequent revisions to collect them under one entry in the thread. Use an explicit `file=` name for extensionless files such as `Dockerfile`, `Makefile`, and `src/LICENSE`. For a patch use a `diff` fence. Preserve the complete file or diff body in each revision. The phone provides a selectable code reader and copy action.

```python file=tools/ready.py
def is_ready(state):
    return state == "ready"
```

```diff file=tools/ready.patch
-    return state == "pending"
+    return state == "ready"
```

Use a reviewed source change and a signed APK release for a new native capability. A proposal can invoke installed actions and rearrange a screen immediately after confirmation.
